{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's groundbreaking experiment with decriminalizing drugs will come to an end on Sunday, as a new law takes effect, making it a crime once again to possess small amounts of hard drugs. Those caught with illicit substances will have the option to either face charges or participate in a treatment program, which includes completing a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Possessing drugs for personal use will be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\nThe new law aims to make it easier for police to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public places like parks. While the law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the criminal justice system, it does not make it mandatory. This has raised concerns that it may create a confusing and inequitable patchwork of policies.\n\nAccording to Portland police chief Bob Day, the criteria for deflection is narrow, and officers will call the county's deflection dispatch line to determine eligibility and connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health outreach team. However, if the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, the person will be taken to jail.\n\nThe new law has sparked debate, with some arguing that it allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources, while others believe it may lead to inconsistent policies.\n\n***", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.03162454441189766}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.11135362833738327}, {"token": " groundbreaking", "logprob": -1.3563698530197144}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -0.04645678028464317}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.26581406593322754}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.000389830